{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t/**\n2\t * cledger's vocabulary over the annals envelope. The envelope itself —\n3\t * identity, serialization, validation — lives in annals and is re-exported\n4\t * here; what this module owns is the set of kinds cledger writes and what\n5\t * they mean. `kind` stays an open string: downstream tools extend the ledger\n6\t * by writing their own kinds, identified by `(producer.tool, kind)`, without\n7\t * a schema release here.\n8\t *\n9\t * `unrecognized` is the one kind cledger emits for content it could *not*\n10\t * interpret: an adapter that meets a transcript line type it has no mapping\n11\t * for preserves the line raw-only under `raw.data` rather than dropping it,\n12\t * so a later adapter version can re-normalize (and supersede) it. Its\n13\t * `content` carries only a `{unrecognized_type}` label — the payload lives in\n14\t * `raw` — but identity still separates distinct lines via `stream.seq`.\n15\t *\n16\t * `reasoning` is for content cledger deliberately never interprets: a\n17\t * provider-encrypted reasoning/thinking blob (e.g. Codex's `reasoning`\n18\t * response_items) that only the originating provider can decrypt. Its\n19\t * `content` carries only an opacity marker; the ciphertext lives in `raw`,\n20\t * for a consumer to opt into replaying back through the same provider.\n21\t * Unlike `unrecognized`, this is not a placeholder awaiting a smarter\n22\t * cledger version — no future version will ever be able to read it either.\n23\t *\n24\t * The four kinds below `reasoning` cover what a source records *around* the\n25\t * conversation. Every one of them was previously discarded at capture as\n26\t * \"bookkeeping\"; they are kept because the ledger's job is the whole record,\n27\t * and because the things a session declares about itself (which model, which\n28\t * sandbox, which worktree) are exactly what a consumer needs to judge whether\n29\t * a turn still applies. They are non-conversational by construction, so\n30\t * `log`/`show` hide them unless asked — recorded always, displayed on request.\n31\t *\n32\t * `session_state` is a *declaration that holds until restated*: the session's\n33\t * mode, permission mode, model settings, sandbox and approval policy, title,\n34\t * worktree or cwd relocation. Sources restate these freely, and each\n35\t * restatement is its own event — \"still true at this point in the transcript\"\n36\t * is a fact, not a duplicate.\n37\t *\n38\t * `activity` is a *point-in-time occurrence that is not a turn*: a hook run,\n39\t * a turn duration, a token count, a task starting or finishing, a queue\n40\t * operation, a context compaction, an aborted turn, a step boundary.\n41\t *\n42\t * `context_injection` is material the harness inserted into the model's\n43\t * context that no participant typed — Claude Code's `attachment` lines\n44\t * (task reminders, skill listings, diagnostics, pasted files). It is content\n45\t * the model read, which is why it is not `activity`, but nobody said it,\n46\t * which is why it is not `conversation_turn`.\n47\t *\n48\t * `file_snapshot` is intermediate file state: the versions a file passed\n49\t * through between commits. Tool calls cannot reconstruct this — a `Bash`\n50\t * mutation records the command, not the result — so without it the record of\n51\t * how a file got from one commit to the next has holes. Sources describe\n52\t * these versions with pointers into a machine-local cache, so the pointers\n53\t * live in `content` and what the ledger could resolve of them at capture time\n54\t * lives in `resolved`.\n55\t */\n56\texport const KNOWN_KINDS = [\n57\t  \"conversation_turn\",\n58\t  \"decision\",\n59\t  \"document\",\n60\t  \"annotation\",\n61\t  \"redaction\",\n62\t  \"supersession\",\n63\t  \"re_anchor\",\n64\t  \"unrecognized\",\n65\t  \"reasoning\",\n66\t  \"session_state\",\n67\t  \"activity\",\n68\t  \"context_injection\",\n69\t  \"file_snapshot\",\n70\t] a